## First read

The page told me that the Supreme Court will decide whether people who entered without admission years ago must remain detained during removal proceedings without a bond hearing.

At the end, I still wanted to know when the Court would hear the case and what the grant means for similar cases now. I first lost the thread at: “The government's petition asked the Court to wait on this case.” This records my first read; it is not a request to change the held petition passage.

## The story

The record supports this story: DHS changed its bond position in July 2025, Barbosa da Cunha was denied a hearing after an arrest inside the country, appeals courts split over the governing statute, and the Supreme Court granted review on October 1, 2026. The page chooses the right opening act. It gives the policy change and the unresolved statutory question, but makes detention sound inevitable even though its [DHS memorandum](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt) says release by parole remains possible. It also leaves the sides of the circuit split out of the story view.

**No later event appeared in the primary dockets checked.** The current [case docket](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-104.html) ends its proceedings with “Petition GRANTED.” and an October 1 filing instruction; the [Lopez-Campos](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/25-1415.html) and [Buenrostro-Mendez](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-43.html) dockets show no later disposition.

The paragraph near the top dates the government’s change, the Board decision, the Second Circuit ruling and the grant. It leaves the denied hearing and subsequent bond release for a later paragraph, after the statutory explanation. The reader must assemble that consequence into the sequence.

| Reader-model answer | Does the marked paragraph carry it? |
| --- | --- |
| 1. What happened | **Yes.** It states the grant and distinguishes it from a ruling. |
| 2. Why it matters | **Partly.** It gives his arrest and release, but overstates what a government win would require and gives no repeatable account of which side the other courts took. |
| 3. Easiest wrong reading | **Yes.** The page separates a grant from a ruling and cases filed from people detained. |
| 4. Mechanism | **Yes.** It explains why the Second Circuit treated “applicant for admission” and “seeking admission” differently, and says a hearing does not itself require release. |
| 5. What changed | **Yes.** The dated paragraph carries the old rule, new position, Board decision, appeal, rehearing denial and grant. |
| 6. What is unresolved | **Yes.** It states that no argument day is listed and distinguishes the granted statutory question from due process. |

## The top

The headline survives the easiest wrong reading, and the first story sentence says what happened. No person is named before introduction. The dek uses “detention statute” and “bond statute” before explaining them, but gives their practical distinction. Its categorical claim that a person is held through the proceedings fails the cold reader because the record allows parole.

## Line edits

1. **Critical, new. FIX:** “If the detention statute applies, a person is held through removal proceedings with no opportunity for release on bond.” The duration is categorical despite the parole exception. The [DHS memorandum](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt) says “may not be released from DHS custody except by INA § 212(d)(5) parole.”

2. **Minor, new. CUT:** “156 days From the Second Circuit's April 28 decision to the grant” The elapsed-day display changes none of the reader’s answers.

3. **Minor, new. CUT:** “in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained” The preceding paragraph explains the clause; the full text belongs in proof.

4. **Minor, new. CUT:** “A bond hearing is a hearing on release.” The surrounding sentences explain what the hearing can and cannot do.

5. **Minor, new. CUT:** “Release in his case followed a finding that he was not a danger and not a flight risk.” The biography paragraph gives the finding and release together.

6. **Major, residual. MOVE:** “On September 26, 2025, officers arrested him while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts. An immigration judge denied a bond hearing. A district court then ordered a bond hearing or release within ten days, and an immigration judge released him on bond after finding no danger and no flight risk.” The early dated sequence needs its consequence. The author added a sequence near the top after the earlier review, but these acts still sit beyond it.

7. **Critical, new. FIX:** “If the government wins, people in his situation are held through the removal process: the Second Circuit wrote that if the detention statute applies, he “must be detained during the removal process” with no opportunity for release on bond.” The page turns the loss of *bond* into unavoidable custody for the entire case. The [DHS memorandum](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt) says “may not be released from DHS custody except by INA § 212(d)(5) parole.”

8. **Major, residual. CLARIFY:** “By the grant the dispute reached far beyond his case: on September 10 the Fourth Circuit wrote that “The debate has now reached every numbered United States court of appeals and has led to a circuit split.”” Reach is stated, but the graded passage identifying the courts on each side never reaches the story view. The earlier review prompted this added quotation; the reader still needs the result in a sentence.

9. **Minor, new. CUT:** “The grant came 156 days after the Second Circuit's decision.” The elapsed time does not change the event or its meaning.

## Holes

1. **Critical. Can someone still leave custody if the government’s reading wins?** The [DHS memorandum](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt) answers: “may not be released from DHS custody except by INA § 212(d)(5) parole.” This changes answers **2 and 4** and displaces the absolute claims in edits **1 and 7**. The page mentions parole later but never reconciles it with those claims.

2. **Major, residual. Which courts took each side?** The [Fourth Circuit opinion](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt) says “The First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh Circuits concluded that Section 1226 applies to noncitizens inside our country”; it says “The Fifth and Eighth Circuits ruled, on the other hand, that Section 1225 applies to all noncitizens who have not been admitted to the United States”. This changes answer **2** and the reach question in answer **7**. It displaces the generic split quotation in edit **8**.

The number detained under this reading and the argument date remain unknown; the page states those limits.

## The budget

About **1,070 words now**. Cuts remove about **90 words**; the two needed clarifications add about **30**. Moving the arrest and release changes placement, not length. Target: roughly **1,010 words**.

## Decision

The two categorical detention claims are Critical against the page’s own DHS record. The incomplete early sequence and missing sides of the split are Major. The cuts make room for the necessary corrections.

VERDICT: NO-SHIP
